22b2dc26 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.026 72.78KB 17.62s
22b2dc26 [grok-build-0.1] #• Thanks for sharing the project state.*
22b2dc26 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc26 [grok-build-0.1] #I've reviewed the files. Here's a concise, high-level breakdown of what stands out:
22b2dc26 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc26 [grok-build-0.1] #Overall Direction
22b2dc26 [grok-build-0.1] #You're building a layered exploration:
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- Low-level: 6502 machine + ROM extraction
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- Mid-level: Centipede-specific emulation + input
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- High-level: Terminal-based visual toys (emoji centipede + Conway grid)
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #This is a coherent arc. The split between the real emulation attempt (`machine.ts`) and the visual
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #demo (`centipede.ts`) is clear.
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #Specific Observations
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #• buildrom.ts*
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- The parser is pragmatic but fragile. It mixes two formats (regular hex lines and `.bulk` lines)
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #in one loop.
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- The `dropCount` and `blankCount` tracking is useful for debugging the source text, but the logic
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #is getting messy with the `break` statements inside the inner loop.
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- The final output loop (printing 32-byte chunks from 0x2000) is handy for verification.
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #• machine.ts*
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- The interpreter is currently a hand-written switch with a very small opcode surface. It will hit
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #the "unsupported opcode" path quickly on real Centipede code.
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- Some nice touches: `tick()` vblank handling, separate `read()` path for the 0x0c00 I/O area, and
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #the `gets8` helper.
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- The `burn()` function and status printing are reasonable for early tracing.
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #- The opcode implementations are inconsistent in how they update `pc` (some use `step()`, some
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #assign directly).
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc27 [grok-build-0.1] #• centipede.ts*
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- The emoji + half-block ship rendering is cute and works surprisingly well in the terminal.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- Shot management is simple but effective.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- Mixing grid drawing with direct `console.log` + cursor codes is getting a bit tangled (especially
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #with the menu wall logic).
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- The FFI import path switch is clean.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #• grid.ts*
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- This is the most developed piece. The multiple window renderers (`gridDotWindowLayer`,
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #`gridQuadWindowLayer`, `gridHalfWindowLayer`, etc.) show good exploration.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- Conway stepping + heatmap + cooling is a nice combination.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- The pump/velocity cursor system is clever.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- Some duplication between the different window functions could be reduced with a more unified
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #approach later.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #• FFI files*
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- `macosffi.ts` and `win32ffi.ts` are appropriately minimal.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- The MIDI callback + queue pattern in the Windows version is solid.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #• Data files*
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- `centipederomv4.json` is well structured (metadata + memory map + binary).
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #- `mos6502.json` is a useful reference, though the current emulator isn't consuming it yet.
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #High-level feedback
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Emulation vs Visual Demo — These two goals are currently fighting each other a bit in
22b2dc28 [grok-build-0.1] #the same directory. Consider whether `machine.ts` should become a proper reusable 6502 core, or
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #whether the visual work should stay in its own "demo" lane.
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Opcode surface — If you want the emulator to actually run meaningful Centipede code,
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #you'll need to expand the interpreter significantly (or switch to a table-driven approach using the
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #`mos6502.json` data).
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Input architecture — There's some duplication between `input.ts`, the FFI files, and how
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #`centipede.ts` / `grid.ts` consume keys. The FFI polling approach is the right direction for low
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #latency.
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like me to focus on any particular area?
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #- Expanding the 6502 interpreter in a maintainable way?
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #- Cleaning up / refactoring one of the terminal renderers?
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #- Improving the ROM extraction or memory map handling?
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #- Something else?
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #Just say what direction interests you most (or if you want a review of a specific file or
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #function).
22b2dc29 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.051 145.7KB 27.13s
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #• Thanks for the update.* This is a noticeable step forward.
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #Here's my assessment of the current state:
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #What Improved
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #• 1. Label support*
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Extracting labels from the disassembly in `buildrom.ts` and embedding them in the JSON is a good
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #move.
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Having real function names in the trace output makes it much more readable.
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #• 2. Emulator progress (`machine.ts`)*
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Significantly more instructions implemented (JSR, RTS, LDY, STY, INY, DEY, more LDA/STA variants,
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #BPL/BMI, CLC, etc.).
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Better status display with the `bits()` visualizer.
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `pc[1]` + `hex()` method for showing raw instruction bytes is useful for debugging.
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Integration with the new `input.ts` (real keyboard polling + ESC to quit).
22b863d1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #• 3. Input handling*
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #- Moving to a queued `startInput`/`pollInput` model is cleaner than the previous approach.
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #• 4. ROM data*
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #- The `labels` section in `centipederomv4.json` is now populated and useful.
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #Issues / Observations
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #• Critical bug: Labels are broken*
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #In `machine.ts`:
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #this.labels = new Map<string, string>();
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #...
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #game.setLabels(rom1.labels);
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #...
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #let label = game.labels[org] || "";
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #`rom1.labels` is a plain object, but you're assigning it to a `Map`. Then you're accessing it with
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #`[]` instead of `.get()`. This will always return `undefined`.
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #You probably want either:
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #- Keep it as a plain object (`Record<string, string>`), or
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #- Properly convert it to a Map in `setLabels()`.
22b863d2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #• Performance / Tracing*
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #You're doing a `console.log` for every single instruction. This is currently the main
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #limiter. Even with the `sleep(20)` every 1024 cycles, this will never feel like emulation.
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #Consider:
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Adding a `--trace` flag or making verbose output optional.
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Only logging on breakpoints or at certain intervals.
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Adding a simple cycle budget + frame counter instead of tracing everything.
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #• I/O Read Spam*
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #if(block==0xc00){
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #    console.log("**0x0c:\t"+hex(address,4)+" "+hex(byte,2));
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #    return byte;
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #}
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #This will fire constantly. Either remove the log or gate it behind a debug flag.
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #• Still missing critical instructions*
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #Centipede will hit several soon:
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #- `ADC`, `SBC`, `CMP`, `CPX`, `CPY`
22b863d3 [grok-build-0.1] #- `ORA`, `BIT`
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #- `INC`, `DEC`
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #- `ROL`, `ROR`, `LSR`
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #- Absolute,Y and (indirect),Y addressing for several instructions
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #• Input is not yet wired to the game*
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #You're reading keys, but not feeding them into `game.input[]`. The game will still see no
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #joystick/fire input.
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #• Minor things*
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #- `this.wordSize=0;` in `tick()` feels odd.
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #- The big `switch` is getting long. At some point a table-driven decoder using `mos6502.json` would
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #be cleaner, but it's fine for now.
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #- `startInput()` is async and runs forever — you correctly poll it, but the architecture means the
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #emulator can't easily run without input being started.
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #Suggested Next Priorities
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Fix the labels Map/object issue (quick win).
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Stop logging every instruction — make tracing optional.
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Wire keyboard input into `game.input[3]` (joystick) and `game.input[1]` (fire).
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Implement the most common missing instructions (especially `CMP`, `ADC`, `INC/DEC`).
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #5. Add a simple "run until frame" or cycle budget mechanism instead of per-instruction
22b863d4 [grok-build-0.1] #tracing.
22b863d5 [grok-build-0.1] #
