22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #Here's some focused feedback on the shared code:
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #1. `bitgrid.js` — Main Issues
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #• Critical Bug in `drawMask`:*
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #const state=char==maskChar;(char=="O");
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #This line is broken. The semicolon makes the second part a no-op. It should probably be:
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bcf [grok-build-0.1] #const state = (char === maskChar) || (char === "O");
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #• Performance Problems*
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #`stepConwayLife` + `countNeighbors` is currently very slow because:
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #- `countNeighbors` calls `getPixel` 8 times per cell.
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #- `getPixel` does wrapping math every time.
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #- `writePixels` is called per row with per-pixel bit manipulation.
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #For a cellular automata grid, this is one of the most expensive patterns.
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #• Recommendations:*
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Add a fast neighbor counting method that works directly on the bit data (especially since you
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #already have `span`).
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Consider double-buffering more explicitly (you already have `readLayer`/`writeLayer`, which is
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #good).
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #- The current `stepConwayLife` allocates a new `pixels` array every row — this can be hoisted.
22cd0bd0 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #• Inconsistency in Wrapping*
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `getPixel` does toroidal wrapping.
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `setPixel`, `rect`, `drawMask`, etc. do not.
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #This is a common source of bugs. You should decide on a consistent policy (either all wrap, or none
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #do, or make it explicit).
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #• Minor Issues*
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `writePixels` has a subtle bug: it reads `word=this.data[offset]` before the loop but
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #only updates `offset` inside the loop under certain conditions. This can cause data corruption at
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #word boundaries.
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `rect()` always sets bits to 1 (no `state` parameter).
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `cool()` and `heat()` are interesting, but `heat()` only works on set bits and walks the data
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #inefficiently.
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd1 [grok-build-0.1] #2. `chargrid.js`
22cd0bd2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd2 [grok-build-0.1] #This is cleaner, but a few notes:
22cd0bd2 [grok-build-0.1] #
22cd0bd2 [grok-build-0.1] #- You're using `Uint32Array` for character codes. That's fine, but consider whether you actually
22cd0bd2 [grok-build-0.1] #need 32 bits or if `Uint16Array` would be sufficient.
22cd0bd2 [grok-build-0.1] #- `toStrings()` is clever, but it will break if any character code is > 0xFFFF (since
22cd0bd2 [grok-build-0.1] #`String.fromCodePoint` can produce surrogate pairs). If you're only using ASCII/emoji in the BMP,
22cd0bd2 [grok-build-0.1] #it's okay.
