2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Understood—discussion only.
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Your existing binary ADC sequence is fundamentally correct:
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `carry = st & 1` produces `0` or `1`, suitable for arithmetic.
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `result = a + value + carry` is the unadjusted binary sum.
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `result > 0xff` correctly determines binary carry-out.
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Your overflow expression is valid for binary addition:
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- overflow occurs when A and operand have the same sign, but the binary result has the opposite
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #sign.
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- Your expression is algebraically equivalent to the more common `~(A ^ operand) & (A ^ result) &
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #$80` form.
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Writing `result` into `Uint8Array` truncates it to eight bits. Explicit masking is clearer but
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #not necessary for binary behavior.
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #For decimal ADC, preserve that binary sum before applying BCD corrections. It is needed for
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #the NMOS 6502’s status behavior:
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- V comes from the binary addition.
2306a7b6 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- On the original NMOS 6502 used in these Atari boards, N and Z also reflect the unadjusted
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #binary result, even though A receives the BCD-adjusted result.
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- C must reflect whether the final decimal addition reaches 100 or above.
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #BCD correction conceptually happens in two stages:
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #1. Add the lower decimal digits—low nibbles plus input carry.
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	If that is greater than 9, add six to correct the invalid binary BCD digit.
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #2. If the combined result is beyond decimal 99, add `$60`.
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	The final overflow beyond eight bits is decimal carry.
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #So, for example:
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `09 + 00 + C=1`
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- Binary sum: `$0A`
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- Low digit is invalid BCD, so correction gives `$10`
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- A becomes `$10`, C clear.
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- NMOS Z is clear because binary `$0A` was nonzero.
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `99 + 01 + C=0`
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- Binary sum: `$9A`
2306a7b7 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- Decimal correction yields `$100`
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- A becomes `$00`, C set.
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #	- NMOS Z is clear, based on binary `$9A`, despite final A being zero.
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #That last point is a well-known NMOS decimal-mode oddity and is the main reason to retain both
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #values: the unadjusted `binaryResult` and the corrected accumulator result.
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Your current `decimal` variable is therefore in exactly the right place. The next change is to
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #choose between the binary path and that BCD-adjusted path after calculating the binary sum, without
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #changing the existing binary behavior.
2306a7b8 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
