23208dad [gpt-5.6-terra] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 gpt-5.6-terra 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.051 58.17KB 12.58s
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #Thanks for sharing this. It is a substantial, hands-on 6502/Atari machine implementation: ROM
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #loading, a partial CPU interpreter, input-mapped I/O, VBLANK IRQ handling, and an early DVG
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #interpreter are all present.
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #A few notable review points:
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Module side effects: ROM selection, input setup, construction, and the main execution
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #loop run at import time. That makes `Machine` and `VectorGenerator` difficult to import into a test
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #harness without launching the emulator. Separating the CLI/bootstrap path from the reusable
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #emulator classes would help.
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Type mismatch in CRT helpers: `buildMemcode()` returns `string[][]`, while `dump()` is
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #declared to accept `Array<Array<number>>`. This should be caught by TypeScript under normal
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #checking.
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #- RTI status restoration appears incorrect: the RTI path masks with `~0x14`, which clears
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #both the Break and Interrupt Disable bits. A real RTI restores I from the stacked status; only
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #Break should generally be treated as non-persistent, while the unused bit remains set. This could
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #create interrupt timing errors.
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Memory-mapped access is inconsistent: some instruction implementations use
23208dae [gpt-5.6-terra] #`readAddress()` / `writeAddress()`, while others access `this.memory[...]` directly. This means I/O
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #behaviour can vary by addressing mode. For example, indirect stores and several absolute
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #read-modify-write operations bypass `writeAddress()`.
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Stack edge cases: JSR directly uses `sp - 1` for the low-byte write. At stack pointer
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #zero, that becomes a negative typed-array index rather than wrapping to `$01FF`. The usual 8-bit
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #stack wrapping needs to be explicit.
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #- CPU timing is intentionally approximate: `tick()` always adds four cycles per
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #instruction. That is fine for bringing the machine up, but VBLANK timing, IRQ placement, and
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #software delay loops will diverge significantly until per-opcode cycle counts and branch/page-cross
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #penalties are accounted for.
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #- DVG protection: DVG call-stack underflow/overflow and malformed vector lists are not
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #guarded, although the overall instruction-count ceiling is a useful safety measure.
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Minor cleanup: there are several currently unused variables/imports (`block`, `index` in
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #`onGo`, `asteroids` is fixed `false`, and a few debug-oriented globals). Those are understandable
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #during active development but may obscure the core execution path.
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #The instruction coverage is already fairly broad for the listed games, and the centralized status
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #helpers (`setStatus`, `setCarry`, `setOverflow`, `adc`, `sbc`) are a good foundation.
23208daf [gpt-5.6-terra] #
