23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 gpt-5.6-terra 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.067 68.71KB 14.94s
23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #A few notable findings from the shared code and trace:
23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #- The Apple II trace reaches `JMP (0036)` with `0036/0037 == 0000`, then BRKs.
23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #  That is why `COUT` lands at `$0000`. The Apple ROM expects monitor vectors such as `COUT` to have
23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #been initialised, or a minimal Apple II firmware/I/O environment to provide them. This is separate
23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #from CPU instruction emulation.
23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6d [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Lunar Lander’s NMI cadence is inconsistent with the frame timing and is likely causing the
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #NMI-handler spin.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  `tick()` is called per interpreted instruction and currently does:
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  - `cycleCount += 4` for every opcode, regardless of actual instruction duration.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  - NMI every `333 * 12 = 3996` nominal cycles.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  - frame duration at `16667` nominal cycles.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  Thus NMIs occur about 4.17 times per displayed frame, and because an NMI is not masked by `I`,
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #another NMI can interrupt an unfinished NMI handler. That can effectively prevent the handler from
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #reaching `RTI`.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  For a 1.5 MHz Atari 6502-family board at 60 Hz, a frame is roughly 25,000 CPU cycles; the
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #interrupt schedule should derive from the selected machine clock and its video timing, rather than
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #the current mixed cycle/microsecond constants.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #- The instruction cycle model needs to precede timing-sensitive emulation.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  A fixed four cycles per instruction will skew interrupts, watchdogs, POKEY timing, and
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #vector-generator polling. At minimum, each opcode should contribute its documented base cycle
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #count, with branch-taken/page-cross penalties where applicable.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `R` reset command supplies the wrong arguments.
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  `Machine.reset` expects:
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  `reset(address, nmi, reset, irq, vectors)`
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #  but the handler calls it with four arguments:
23587a6e [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #  `game.reset(0x2000, 0x38c0, 0x3b4b, game.dvg.enabled);`
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #  So `game.dvg.enabled` becomes the IRQ address and `vectors` is `undefined`. The regular startup
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #call is correct.
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Interrupt status-stack behaviour is slightly inaccurate.
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #  For IRQ/NMI, the pushed status should have the break bit clear and the unused bit set. BRK should
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #push break set. The current generic `interrupt()` pushes `st` unchanged. This may not explain the
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #current spin, but software can inspect the stacked flags.
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #- Several instructions bypass `writeAddress()` or `readAddress()`.
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #  For example, `STA ($nn),Y`, some `INC`/`DEC`/shift absolute forms, and numerous zero-page reads
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #use direct `memory[...]`. That works for RAM but bypasses I/O side effects. Once the Apple II soft
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #switches or Atari device registers are modelled, those paths will need to go through the bus access
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #functions.
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #- The Battlezone watchdog spin at `$7B71` is expected ROM behaviour.
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #  The code in `notes.txt` explicitly loops until a watchdog reset after detecting stack
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #overflow/underflow. If execution arrives there, it is useful evidence of an earlier stack or
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #interrupt-return issue, rather than the root cause itself.
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #For Lunar Lander, I would first make NMI generation frame-based and ensure only one NMI is raised
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #per intended vblank. Then verify that the handler returns via `RTI` before investigating ROM logic.
23587a6f [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587e71 [simon@midnightblue.local] explain apple2 init for COUT vector
23587f73 [gpt-5.6-terra] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 gpt-5.6-terra 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.075 72.06KB 15.79s
23587f73 [gpt-5.6-terra] #`COUT` is an indirect output entry point in the Apple II monitor ROM.
23587f73 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f73 [gpt-5.6-terra] #In the trace:
23587f73 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f73 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f73 [gpt-5.6-terra] #FD8E  CROUT  LDA #$8D
23587f73 [gpt-5.6-terra] #FD90         BNE FDED
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #FDED  COUT   JMP ($0036)
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #`$FDED` does not itself write a character. It jumps through the 16-bit zero-page vector at:
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #$0036  CSWL   low byte of character-output routine
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #$0037         high byte
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #So, with `0036 = 00` and `0037 = 00`, this instruction becomes:
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #JMP ($0036)  →  JMP $0000
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Then `$0000` contains `BRK` because your freshly allocated RAM is zero-filled.
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Why the vector is zero
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Your emulator begins Apple II execution at the ROM’s BASIC entry point:
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f74 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #E000: JSR F000
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #That skips the normal Apple II monitor reset/bootstrap sequence. On real hardware, reset begins at
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #the address held in `FFFC/FFFD`, which enters the monitor reset code. That code establishes the
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #zero-page monitor workspace, including the I/O dispatch vectors.
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #In particular, it installs the default character output routine—normally the monitor’s
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #screen-output routine, often called `COUT1`—into `CSWL` at `36/37`.
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Conceptually, reset performs something equivalent to:
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #CSWL = address of monitor default output routine
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #KSWL = address of monitor default keyboard-input routine
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #where:
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #CSWL = $0036
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #KSWL = $0038
23587f75 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #The exact routine addresses vary somewhat by Apple II ROM revision, so the correct values should
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #come from the monitor reset path in the ROM image rather than being treated as universal constants.
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Why it is a vector rather than a direct call
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #The monitor exposes stable public entry points such as:
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `COUT` — output the character in `A`
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `RDKEY` — read a key
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `CROUT` — emit carriage return
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- `HOME`, `CLEAR`, and similar monitor services
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Programs call `COUT` at `FDED`, but the actual destination can be replaced by changing `36/$37`.
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #This lets software redirect output to, for example:
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- the normal text display,
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- a serial card,
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- a printer,
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- a custom terminal routine,
23587f76 [gpt-5.6-terra] #- another display mode.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #So BASIC expects `COUT` to work because the monitor reset code has already assigned `CSWL`.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Appropriate emulator startup sequence
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #For the Apple II configuration, emulate a power-on/reset sequence rather than entering directly at
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #BASIC `$E000`.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #1. Load RAM and ROMs.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #2. Read the reset vector at `FFFC/FFFD`.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #3. Set `PC` to that address.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #4. Execute the monitor reset routine.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #5. Let it initialize monitor workspace and I/O vectors.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #6. Allow that code to enter BASIC naturally.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Starting directly at `E000` is useful for debugging BASIC, but it requires reproducing the
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #monitor’s expected initialized state—especially `36/$37`—first.
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Minimal diagnostic
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Before the `JMP ($0036)` in your trace, inspect:
23587f77 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #$0036, $0037
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #$0038, $0039
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #They should contain valid monitor-ROM addresses after a genuine reset. In the current run they are
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #zero because `Machine` creates zeroed RAM and the monitor initialization path has not run.
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #Once `CSWL` is valid, the next issue will be that the default Apple II output routine accesses
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #soft-switch addresses and writes text into screen RAM. Those accesses need appropriate Apple II
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #memory-mapped I/O behaviour, notably for display/keyboard-related soft switches, for visible
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #console output rather than merely successful execution.
23587f78 [gpt-5.6-terra] #
