2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.003 63.95KB 28.13s
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #This is an impressive and substantial 6502 emulator project, managing multiple classic arcade and
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #home computer ROMs within a single TypeScript codebase. The `VectorEngine` class is a standout
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #feature—it implements a portion of the vector graphics display list processing used in games like
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #Asteroids and Battlezone, which is rarely seen in hobby emulators. That’s genuinely novel and
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #well-executed.
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #A few observations and potential improvements:
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #Outstanding points
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #- DVG opcode parsing (VEC, LABS, SVEC, JSRL, RTSL, JMP, HALT) is cleanly separated and
2374b75f [deepseek-v4-flash] #works with the $3000 “go” signal.
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Apple II text display via `displayApple` is straightforward and ties directly to the
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #$C0xx keyboard emulation.
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The main loop uses async I/O (`pollInput`, `sleep`) without blocking, making it suitable
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #for Deno’s runtime.
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Single‑stepping (`step`, `stepping`) plus breakpoints provides a reasonable debugging workflow.
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Areas to watch
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. Reset call mismatch
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   `game.reset(0x2000, 0x38c0, 0x3b4b, game.dvg.enabled);` passes only four arguments, but the
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #method signature expects five (including `vectors:boolean`). The fifth argument is missing, so
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`irq` gets set to the vector engine enabled flag. This likely breaks the Apple II reset path.
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Should be:
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   `game.reset(0x2000, 0x38c0, 0x3b4b, 0x3b4b, false);` (or whatever vectors you intend).
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Cycle accounting
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   `tick()` increments `this.cycleCount` by 4 every instruction, but real 6502 cycles vary per
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #opcode. For accurate timing (especially for VBLANK and NMI generation), consider storing actual
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #cycles per opcode in a lookup table.
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #3. Missing opcodes
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   The large `switch` in `interpret6502` covers most common opcodes, but many addressing modes are
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #still missing (e.g., `0xB6` LDX ZP,Y, `0x96` STX ZP,Y, `0x3D` AND abs,X, `0x1E` ASL abs,X, etc.).
2374b760 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Some games may crash on unrecognized opcodes.
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #4. Input handling global state
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   `stepping`, `step`, and `game` are global variables. For a more testable design, you might
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #encapsulate the emulator loop inside the `Machine` class or a separate controller.
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #5. Performance
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   The main loop prints `"."` every 100ms when not stepping—this is fine. But stepping prints one
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #line per instruction, which can flood the console. Consider throttling or a pager mode.
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #6. Vector engine completeness
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   The DVG class currently only handles a subset of opcodes. The Atari vector games also use
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`SIGN`, `OFFSET`, and possibly `CNT`. The parser returns control to the CPU after `HALT`, which is
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #correct, but be aware that many games rely on the DVG list being re‑entrant.
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #What’s new or different
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The combined Apple II + Atari vector emulation in a single file with runtime ROM
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #selection is unusual and shows a nice design for reuse.
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The `memCode` function for displaying Atari playfield codes (damage, mushrooms, etc.) is a clever
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #mapping that makes debugging much easier.
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The use of `JSON` imports for ROM dumps (with `with { type: "json" }`) is modern and works well
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #with Deno.
2374b761 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Suggestions for next steps
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Add a cycle-accurate opcode table to replace the manual `cycleCount+=4`.
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Implement the remaining 6502 opcodes (especially the less common ones like `0xB6`, `0x9E`,
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`0x1A`, etc.).
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Verify the DVG interrupt timing (NMI at 33 kHz) matches the hardware spec for vector refresh.
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Consider adding a simple disassembler view that can be toggled on/off during stepping.
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Overall, a solid foundation for a retro‑computing emulator with some unique features. Keep it up!
2374b762 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
