2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The backspace key behavior is typical of Apple II emulation. The issue is that the emulator pushes
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the raw keycode `8` (which is indeed the Apple II keyboard code for backspace) into the game's key
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #buffer via `game.pushKey(8)`. The game's keyboard‑read routine (typically polling `$C000` for
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #keypresses) will then fetch that value. Many Apple II programs, especially games, do not
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #interpret the backspace character as a cursor‑movement/erase command—instead, they treat it as a
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #printable character and write it directly into the text screen (or use it in a numeric input
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #routine).
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Why “odd characters” appear
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #In the Apple II character set, code `08` corresponds to a graphic symbol (usually a small square or
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #a blank) because the ROM font maps many control codes to glyphs. When the game stores `08` into
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #screen RAM, that symbol appears. The backspace “works” in the sense that the game decrements the
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #cursor position or deletes a previous character, but then it also writes that control character
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #into the screen buffer, leaving a visible artifact.
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #How to fix it
2374c714 [deepseek-v4-flash] #There are two common approaches:
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. Translate the backspace key to a space at the previous screen position
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Instead of pushing raw keycode `8`, you can simulate the backspace effect by directly modifying
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the screen memory. For example, when a backspace is received, you could:
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   - Decrement the cursor position (if the game uses a memory‑based cursor variable—but that’s
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #game‑dependent).
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   - Write a space (`0x20 | 0x80` for normal display) at that location.
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   - Then push nothing (or a dummy key) so the game doesn’t try to read it.
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   This is fragile because games manage their own cursor pointers.
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Use the correct key mapping expected by the game
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Many Apple II games expect Clr (clear) or Delete (`FF`) to erase the character
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #left of the cursor, while backspace (`08`) is often reserved for the firmware’s line‑editing
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #routines. If you map Delete (keycode 127 on modern keyboards) to `FF` and backspace (key 8) to
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`08`, the game might treat it as a regular character.
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   - Check the game’s source code (if available) or experiment: try pushing `00`, `7F`, or `$DF`
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #(left‑arrow) instead.
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Suggested modification to your code
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #In the `runMachine` loop, you can change the backspace handling:
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c715 [deepseek-v4-flash] #case 127: // Delete key (modern keyboard)
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  // Many Apple II games use $7F (Delete) or $DF (Left Arrow)
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  // Try: game.pushKey(0x7F);
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  // Or simply ignore it if you want backspace to erase a character in your own display helper.
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  break;
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #case 8:  // Backspace
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  // Option: push $08 (standard backspace)
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  game.pushKey(8);
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  break;
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you want the backspace to actually erase the last character on screen (for a more “native”
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #feel), you’d need to modify the game’s keyboard handler or implement a custom screen manager—both
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #are non‑trivial.
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Quick test
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Print the keycode the game receives by adding a diagnostic in `readAddress` when reading `$C000`:
2374c716 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #case 0xc000:
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  let key = this.keyState;
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  if (((key & 0x80) == 0) && this.keyBuffer.length) {
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #    key = this.keyBuffer.shift() | 0x80;
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #    this.keyState = key;
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #    console.log(`Keyboard input: 0x${key.toString(16)}`);  // << ADD THIS
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  }
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  return key;
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Then press backspace and see what value the game picks up. That will tell you exactly which code
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the game is seeing, and you can decide whether to change the mapping or adjust the emulator
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #accordingly.
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Let me know if you’d like a more detailed walkthrough of Apple II keyboard scanning or a fix that
2374c717 [deepseek-v4-flash] #works for a specific game ROM.
