2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #The “hex monitor” is likely the Apple II built-in firmware monitor (the one you enter via `CALL
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #-151` or that appears in some ROMs). That monitor uses a specific keyboard protocol: it
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #expects `Delete` (keycode `7F`) to erase the last character, while `Backspace` (`08`) is treated as
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #a literal control character that prints a glyph (e.g., a small square).
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #When you press the physical Backspace key (which your code maps to keycode `8`), the emulator
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #pushes `08` into the key buffer. The monitor reads it, writes it to screen RAM (because it echoes
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #all characters), and that’s the “odd character” you see—it’s the glyph for `08` in the Apple II
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #character set.
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9c [deepseek-v4-flash] #The Fix
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #Change the backspace mapping in the main event loop to send Delete (`7F`) instead of `08`.
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #Many classic Apple II programs (including the monitor) treat `$7F` as “erase previous character”.
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #In your `switch(key)` block, replace:
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #case 127:
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #  game.pushKey(8);
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #  break;
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #with:
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #case 8:   // Backspace → send Delete ($7F)
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #  game.pushKey(0x7F);
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #  break;
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #(If you also use Delete key for its own purpose, you can map both to `$7F` or add a separate
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #mapping.)
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #Why This Works
2374ca9d [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The monitor checks for `$7F` (128 decimal) when processing backspace, and it actually decrements
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #the cursor position and writes a space.
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `$08` (8 decimal) is ignored or printed as a glyph.
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #If the Monitor Still Shows Odd Characters
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #Some monitors (like the Apple II+ / IIe firmware) also accept `DF` (left arrow) for backspace. You
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #can try `0xDF` (223 decimal). But `7F` is the standard Delete key code for the Apple II keyboard
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #(hardware key position D2).
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #After making the change, restart the emulator, enter the monitor (e.g., by pressing `Ctrl+B`,
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #`Reset`, or through a direct `CALL -151` if your game ROM supports it), and press Backspace. The
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #monitor should now erase characters normally.
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #Let me know if you need help identifying the exact ROM’s keyboard handler or a more robust
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #input-remapping solution.
2374ca9e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
