2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- When you type in the monitor, it reads a key (`$C000`), then calls `COUT` to display it.
2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `COUT` (at `FDF0` in standard Apple II firmware) checks for `08` (backspace): it decrements the
2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #cursor pointer, writes a space, then decrements again. However, the monitor’s input routine
2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #also immediately stores the character to screen memory before calling `COUT` on some ROM variants.
2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Or your ROM might use a different routine that doesn’t call `COUT` for control characters.
2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The `displayApple` function reads raw screen memory, so any byte written to `400` shows up,
2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #including the glyph for `08`.
2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374ec27 [deepseek-v4-flash] #A direct emulator‑side fix
2374ec28 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Instead of fighting the ROM’s behavior, you can simulate backspace at the emulator level by
2374ec28 [deepseek-v4-flash] #directly manipulating the Apple II’s text cursor and screen memory. This makes the monitor (and any
2374ec28 [deepseek-v4-flash] #program that uses the standard cursor at `24`/`25`) work correctly without changing key mappings.
