2374eecd [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 7 set (e.g., `C1` for inverse "A") becomes `41` and displays as normal "A". That’s fine for
2374eecd [deepseek-v4-flash] #normal text, but when the monitor writes a raw backspace code (`08`), it has bit 7 clear, and
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #masking still gives `08`—a control character that appears as a glyph (like a small square or
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #underscore).
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you want inverse uppercase to appear as actual visual alternates, you’d need a display routine
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #that respects bit 7 (e.g., drawing with a background color). But most home‑brewn Apple II emulators
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #just treat the high bit as “inverted” and render accordingly.
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #In short: Yes, to get a lowercase‑like appearance, programmers used inverse uppercase, but
2374eece [deepseek-v4-flash] #your current display function ignores the inverse flag.
