2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #On an original Apple II (II/II+), the video memory at `$400` stores 7‑bit character codes
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #with an 8th bit (bit 7) that toggles the display mode:
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Bit 7 = 0 → Normal (white on black)
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Bit 7 = 1 → Inverse (black on white)
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #Most system software (including the monitor) writes bytes with bit 7 set by default,
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #because the inverse mode gives better contrast on many monitors. The character itself (bits 0–6) is
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #the standard Apple II character code (uppercase letters, numbers, punctuation, and graphic
2374fd2d [deepseek-v4-flash] #symbols).
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #Your current `displayApple` does:
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #let charCode = (byte & 0x7f) || 32;
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #This strips bit 7 and then treats the code as a plain ASCII/Apple II character. Because the high
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit is lost, everything appears as normal text, and you never see the intended inverse
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #display (e.g., the monitor prompt, cursor, etc.). The backspace code `08` has bit 7 clear, so after
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #masking it stays `08` and prints a glyph.
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #To properly emulate the Apple II text screen:
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. Use `byte & 0x7F` to get the character code.
2374fd2e [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Use `byte & 0x80` as an inverse flag.
