23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.005 87.82KB 33.86s
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #It sounds like the hex digit `0` is being displayed as the letter `P` when you type it. This
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #indicates a mismatch between the keycode your emulator sends and the code the Apple II
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #monitor expects.
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Most common cause: you are sending a scan code instead of an ASCII code
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The original Apple II keyboard does not produce ASCII codes directly. It returns a
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #key‑matrix scan code (a 7‑bit value) with bit 7 set. The firmware (at `$FD18` or similar)
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #then translates that scan code into the ASCII character that appears on screen.
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #If your emulator bypasses that translation (i.e., you call `pushKey` with an ASCII value like
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`0x30` for `0`), then the monitor will see that ASCII value as if it were a scan code. The
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #ASCII `0x30` happens to be the scan code for the `P` key on the Apple II keyboard (key position
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`$30` = `P` on the original matrix). So the monitor displays a `P`.
23750d06 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #How to fix
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #You have two options:
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Option 1: Let the firmware translate (recommended)
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Instead of sending raw ASCII codes, send the Apple II scan codes for the keys. For example:
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `0` key → scan code `0B` (or `8B` with strobe)
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `1` key → scan code `$0C`
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `2` key → scan code `$0D`
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- etc.
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #You can find the full scan‑code table for the Apple II keyboard. Then the monitor’s own ROM routine
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #will convert them to the correct ASCII characters and your problem disappears.
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Option 2: Directly write to screen memory
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you want to keep sending ASCII codes, you must also bypass the monitor’s input handler and
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #directly place the character into screen RAM at the cursor position. That’s more work and fragile.
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Quick diagnosis: add a debug print
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Add this temporary logging in `readAddress` at `$C000`:
23750d07 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #case 0xc000:
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  let key = this.keyState;
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  if (((key & 0x80) == 0) && this.keyBuffer.length) {
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #    key = this.keyBuffer.shift() | 0x80;
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #    this.keyState = key;
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #    console.log(`[KBD] reading $C000 → 0x${key.toString(16)}`);
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  }
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #  return key;
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Then press `0` and look at the console.
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- If you see `0xB0` (ASCII `0` with bit 7 set), the monitor should display an inverse `0`. If it
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #shows `P` instead, the monitor is interpreting `0xB0` as a scan code (which equals the key for `P`
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #on the Apple II).
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- If you see a different value (e.g., `0x0B`), you are already sending a scan code, but maybe the
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #wrong one.
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Recommended fix for Option 1
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Change your key mapping to send Apple II scan codes:
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
23750d08 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
