237c1b5c [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 0.7° $0.132 63.96KB 209.37s
237c1b5c [kimi-k3] #Thanks for sharing — this is a substantial update. A few comments, roughly grouped:
237c1b5c [kimi-k3] #
237c1b5c [kimi-k3] #What's new or notable
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #- Apple II is now the live target (`gameRom=apple2`), with a proper text-page renderer. The row
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #interleave in `displayApple` (`0x400 + 40(y>>3) + 128(y&7)`) matches real text page 1
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #layout exactly.
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #- Keyboard handling at `C000`/`C010` follows hardware convention — strobe bit set on read when a
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #key is pending, cleared by any access to `C010`. Speaker toggle stub at `C030` is in place.
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #- The DVG opcode map in `VectorEngine` (LABS/VEC/SVEC/JSRL/RTSL/HALT at 0xA–0xF) matches the Atari
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #vector generator, and `JMP Indirect` (0x6C) faithfully reproduces the original 6502 page-boundary
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #wrap bug — a nice authenticity touch.
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #- BCD paths in `adc`/`sbc` are present, and the `sbc` borrow/overflow derivation looks correct.
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #Possible issues
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #1. `reportMissing` prints wrong values. `Object.keys(missingCodes)` returns strings, and
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #`String.toString(16)` ignores the radix, so `hex(code, 2)` echoes the decimal key rather than
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #converting it. A `parseInt(code, 10)` before formatting would fix it.
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #2. Inverse video polarity in `displayApple`. On real hardware, bit 7 set (80–FF) is normal
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #text; 00–3F is inverse and 40–7F flashes. The current code renders bit-7-set as inverse, which is
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #inverted relative to hardware — unless that's a deliberate aesthetic choice.
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #3. `interrupt()` clears `stepping`. Any taken interrupt — including the first vblank IRQ
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #after a CLI — halts the machine. Handy as a debug trap, but it will stop raster-mode execution the
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #moment the ROM enables interrupts. Worth confirming that's still intended.
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #4. Inconsistent write paths. `INC Absolute` (0xEE), `DEC Absolute` (0xCE), and `LSR
237c1b5d [kimi-k3] #Absolute` (0x4E) write via `this.memory[address]=` directly, bypassing `writeAddress` and therefore
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #the screen-RAM dirty flag — unlike their X-indexed counterparts, which do route through
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #`writeAddress`. An `INC $4xx` would silently miss a redraw.
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #5. Swapped comments on key handlers. `case 100` is lowercase 'd' and `case 68` is uppercase
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #'D' — the `//D` / `//d` comments are reversed.
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #Housekeeping observations
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #- `updatePumps`/`fadePumps`/`pump` appear to be leftover thruster code — nothing calls them now.
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #- `select` is imported but unused since ROM selection is commented out.
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #- The `""+` prefix in `hex()` is redundant.
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #- `missingCodes`, `keyBuffer`, and a few parameters (`keyCode`, `offset`) are untyped; the rest of
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #the file is fairly well annotated, so these stand out.
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #- BRK halting the machine reads as a deliberate debug breakpoint — fine, just noting it alongside
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #the interrupt behaviour above.
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #The fixed 4-cycles-per-instruction placeholder in `tick()` is clearly marked, so I assume accurate
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #cycle counting is on the roadmap. Overall the structure is holding up well for a dual-architecture
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #(vector/raster) emulator — the main thing I'd watch is the Machine class accumulating game-specific
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #behaviour (the Centipede sprite overlay in `refreshCRT`, Apple II soft switches) as more titles
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #come online.
237c1b5e [kimi-k3] #
