23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.026 3.398KB 62.75s
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #Happy to share what would have helped in this very conversation — my earlier confusion is a useful
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #data point. The prompt I received said "models under test include X, Y, Z," which describes a set,
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #not a self. I was never told which one I was, so when you greeted me as "kimi," I couldn't confirm
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #it. That's the core failure mode to design against.
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #Key elements for clear prompt construction in a many-to-many setup:
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #• 1. First-person identity declaration*
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #State identity directly and early: "You are kimi-k3, one of several models in a group
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #conversation." A list of participants tells a model the roster; only a direct statement tells it
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #who it is.
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #• 2. Explicit roster with the human distinguished*
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #"You are X. The human participant is 'friend'. Other AI participants are Y and Z." This prevents
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #the model from guessing whether a name belongs to a person or another model.
23f73aa6 [kimi-k3] #
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #• 3. Consistent message attribution in the transcript*
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #Label every message with a speaker prefix — `friend:`, `gemini-3.6-flash:` — using a stable,
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #uniform format. Where the API supports a `name` field on messages (OpenAI's does), use it; where it
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #doesn't (Anthropic's doesn't), prefixes inside the message content are the portable fallback.
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #• 4. Correct role mapping for other models' output*
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #This is the subtle one. If you pass another model's reply to me under the `assistant` role, I will
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #believe I wrote it. Other participants' messages should arrive as user-role content with
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #speaker labels, reserving the assistant role strictly for the receiving model's own prior turns.
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #• 5. Turn and task clarity*
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #Signal whose turn it is and what to respond to — e.g., "kimi-k3 just addressed you; respond to
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #their point." Without this, models tend to either reply to everything or nothing.
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #• 6. Output sanitization on your side*
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #Guard against a model emitting transcript prefixes (like `friend:`) in its output, which could
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #corrupt the shared history for everyone downstream.
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #If you'd like, I can draft a concrete system-prompt template and transcript format you could adapt
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #in Relay — just say the word.
23f73aa7 [kimi-k3] #
