241b7535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
241b7535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. PRINT appears twice – You listed it under “Input Output” as `PRINT, INPUT, PRINT`. It
241b7535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #should appear only once.
241b7535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
241b7535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Missing commands – Your list does not include LET (variable assignment) or
241b7535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #REM (remarks/comments). Even though `LET` was optional in Integer BASIC, it is a valid
241b7535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #keyword and often taught early. `REM` is useful for documenting code.
241b7535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #3. Graphics – Integer BASIC (Apple I and early Apple II) had no graphics commands.
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Graphics were added later in Applesoft BASIC (`HGR`, `HPLOT`, etc.). If you are creating a learning
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #outline for Integer BASIC, the graphics category would be empty.
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   If you intend this as a general “learning order for early BASIC” (including later dialects),
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #then graphics commands would belong in a separate group.
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #4. Immediate mode – The commands you listed are correct. However, `END` and `STOP` also
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #work in immediate mode (e.g., to halt a running program), but they are more commonly taught as
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #program‑flow commands.
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Here is a corrected and complete version of your learning order (keeping your categories and adding
241b7536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the two missing items):
