241b864f [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.012 11.51KB 18.19s
241b864f [kimi-k3] #Yes—there was an Apple I Integer BASIC.
241b864f [kimi-k3] #
241b864f [kimi-k3] #The key distinction is:
241b864f [kimi-k3] #
241b864f [kimi-k3] #- Apple I Integer BASIC
241b864f [kimi-k3] #  - Written by Steve Wozniak.
241b864f [kimi-k3] #  - Very small, tape-oriented BASIC.
241b864f [kimi-k3] #  - No Apple II-specific graphics commands like `GR`, `COLOR`, `PLOT`.
241b864f [kimi-k3] #
241b864f [kimi-k3] #- Apple II Integer BASIC
241b864f [kimi-k3] #  - Descended from / adapted from the Apple I version.
241b864f [kimi-k3] #  - Added support for Apple II features, especially low-resolution graphics and related I/O.
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #  - This is why Brick Out / Little Brick Out could be Integer BASIC on the Apple II.
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #So if someone says “Integer BASIC,” the right follow-up is often: “Apple I or Apple II?”
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #The command set differs enough that answers can otherwise sound contradictory.
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #A cautious rule of thumb:
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #- Core integer-only language: shared heritage
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #- Apple II extensions: graphics and machine-specific features
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #So your earlier markdown makes much more sense if it is specifically for Apple II Integer
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #BASIC, not the Apple I version.
241b8650 [kimi-k3] #
