241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #Good question to double-check, because the dates are close together and easy to blur:
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #- The Apple I shipped in 1976.
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #- The Apple II shipped in 1977.
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #So a "1977 manual" for Integer BASIC would be an Apple II document. The relevant one is
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #usually cited as the Apple II BASIC Programming Manual (1977) — it covers Integer BASIC as
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #shipped on the Apple II, including the graphics commands we discussed.
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #That said, I'm relying on memory for the exact title and year. The things I'm confident about:
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #- Apple II = 1977, Apple I = 1976. Solid.
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #- The early Apple II programming manual documented Integer BASIC, since Applesoft didn't arrive
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #until 1978 (on cassette, then in ROM with the II Plus). Solid.
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #- So: yes, a 1977 Integer BASIC manual is Apple II material.
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #The thing I'm less certain about is the precise title — it may be Apple II BASIC Programming
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #Manual or similar phrasing, and there were also the "Red Book" (the January 1978 Apple II
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #Reference Manual, often called the red book) which contains the Integer BASIC monitor/ROM
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #details.
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #
241bbb88 [kimi-k3] #For your purposes, the useful primary sources to hunt down would be:
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #1. The 1977/78 Apple II BASIC manual — keyword syntax and semantics as shipped.
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #2. Wozniak's own description of Integer BASIC — he wrote about it in early Apple documentation and
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #later in interviews; useful for design intent.
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #3. The disassembled ROM listing — the ground truth for your emulator.
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #If you want, I can suggest what specific test cases to extract once you have one of those in hand,
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #but I'd rather not assert page numbers or section titles I can't verify.
241bbb89 [kimi-k3] #
