241df482 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.141 127.0KB 104.97s
241df482 [kimi-k3] #Two files this time — and `machine.ts` answers a lot of questions about the project. Comments on
241df482 [kimi-k3] #each.
241df482 [kimi-k3] #
241df482 [kimi-k3] #integerbasic.md
241df482 [kimi-k3] #
241df482 [kimi-k3] #The five stragglers (`POP`, `TRACE`, `NOTRACE`, `PR#`, `IN#`) are appended at the bottom without a
241df482 [kimi-k3] #home. Suggested placement:
241df482 [kimi-k3] #
241df483 [kimi-k3] #- POP → Program Flow. It discards the most recent `GOSUB` return address, so it's stack
241df483 [kimi-k3] #manipulation — same family as `GOSUB`/`RETURN`.
241df483 [kimi-k3] #- TRACE / NOTRACE → a small "Debugging" section, or fold into Immediate Mode. Worth noting
241df483 [kimi-k3] #these are Integer BASIC only — Applesoft dropped them, so they're a nice fingerprint of the
241df483 [kimi-k3] #dialect.
241df483 [kimi-k3] #- PR# / IN# → Fundamentals or their own "Device Redirection" note. They reroute
241df483 [kimi-k3] #output/input to a slot (e.g., `PR#1` for a printer card). On a bare machine they're mostly no-ops
241df483 [kimi-k3] #or hangs.
241df483 [kimi-k3] #
241df483 [kimi-k3] #machine.ts
241df483 [kimi-k3] #
241df483 [kimi-k3] #This is the first look at the actual emulator — a Deno-based 6502 interpreter covering the Atari
241df483 [kimi-k3] #vector titles (Lunar Lander, Asteroids, Centipede, Battlezone via the DVG) and now Apple II/II+.
241df483 [kimi-k3] #Impressively compact for what it does. A few things that matter for the Integer BASIC goal:
241df483 [kimi-k3] #
241df483 [kimi-k3] #• 1. Soft-switch writes will halt the machine (the blocker for GR).*
241df483 [kimi-k3] #In `writeAddress`, any write to `C001`–`C0FF` logs and sets `stepping=false`. But Apple II soft
241df483 [kimi-k3] #switches respond to any access, read or write — and Integer BASIC's `GR` statement
241df483 [kimi-k3] #performs `STA C050`. So the first graphics command in Brick Out will stop the emulator. The fix is
241df483 [kimi-k3] #to route writes to `C050`–`$C05F` through the same handler as reads (even if the handler is
241df483 [kimi-k3] #currently stubbed), rather than treating them as errors.
241df483 [kimi-k3] #
241df483 [kimi-k3] #• 2. `$C052` is missing from the read switch.*
241df483 [kimi-k3] #Mixed-mode-off (the full 40×48 screen, no 4-line text window) is accessed at `C052` — Brick Out-era
241df483 [kimi-k3] #programs commonly touch it. Right now a read falls through to the ` C0XX` halt. Add it
241df484 [kimi-k3] #alongside `C050`/`C051`.
241df484 [kimi-k3] #
241df484 [kimi-k3] #• 3. No lores renderer yet.*
241df484 [kimi-k3] #`displayApple` renders the text screen only. Lores is the same `400`–`7FF` memory,
241df484 [kimi-k3] #reinterpreted: each byte is two stacked blocks, low nybble on top, high nybble on bottom, 40×48.
241df484 [kimi-k3] #Since you're already in a terminal, upper/lower half-block characters (`▀` with
241df484 [kimi-k3] #foreground/background colours) would map onto this naturally — one terminal row per byte-row,
241df484 [kimi-k3] #16-colour ANSI foreground/background per nybble.
241df484 [kimi-k3] #
241df484 [kimi-k3] #• 4. ROM selection: you want `apple2`, not `apple2plus`, for Integer BASIC.*
241df484 [kimi-k3] #The II Plus autostart ROM boots into Applesoft. Your `roms` array currently selects `apple2plus`;
241df484 [kimi-k3] #`apple2` is the Integer BASIC machine. You have both imported already, so it's a one-line flip —
241df484 [kimi-k3] #and a nice A/B test since Applesoft boots differently (its `]` prompt vs Integer BASIC's `>`
241df484 [kimi-k3] #prompt).
241df484 [kimi-k3] #
241df484 [kimi-k3] #• 5. Minor: IRQ vectors to the Reset address.*
241df484 [kimi-k3] #In `interrupt()`, the non-NMI path loads the vector from `FFFC` (Reset), but IRQ should use `FFFE`.
241df484 [kimi-k3] #Also, the 6502 sets the I flag on IRQ entry, not just NMI. This is currently dormant (the IRQ path
241df484 [kimi-k3] #is commented out), so it's a note for later rather than a live bug.
241df484 [kimi-k3] #
241df484 [kimi-k3] #• 6. ROM write-protection.*
241df484 [kimi-k3] #Memory is flat writable RAM, including the burned ROM regions. Fine for now, but a stray Integer
241df484 [kimi-k3] #BASIC `POKE` into `$E000`+ will silently corrupt the interpreter. A range check in `writeAddress`
241df484 [kimi-k3] #would turn those into log events instead of confusing behaviour.
241df484 [kimi-k3] #
241df484 [kimi-k3] #• 7. Flash/inverse logic in `displayApple` looks right.*
241df485 [kimi-k3] #The `0x40`–`0x7F` flash window gated on `frameCount & 64` matches the original Apple II charset
241df485 [kimi-k3] #behaviour.
241df485 [kimi-k3] #
241df485 [kimi-k3] #The cassette WAV round-trip (record cycle deltas, emit 22050 Hz WAV, re-ingest at startup) is a
241df485 [kimi-k3] #tidy design — and once `LOAD`/`SAVE` work, that's the path to getting period tape images of Integer
241df485 [kimi-k3] #BASIC programs into the machine without typing them.
241df485 [kimi-k3] #
