241dff1f [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.120 131.0KB 28.16s
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #Yes, that's exactly right — and it's good news for your emulation accuracy.
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #The original Apple II (non-Plus, 1977) shipped with the "Monitor ROM" (also called the "old
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #monitor" or "System Monitor"). On power-up/reset it drops you into the Monitor at the `*` prompt.
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #From there:
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl-B (then Enter) → enters Integer BASIC, giving you the `>` prompt.
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl-C → re-enter the currently resident BASIC (warm start).
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #- Monitor commands like `E000G` would also work, since Integer BASIC lives at `$E000`.
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #The behaviour you're describing — boot to `*`, then Ctrl-B, Enter, then `>` — is the authentic
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #power-on experience of a 1977 Apple II with Integer BASIC in ROM. It was the Apple II Plus
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #(1978) whose Autostart ROM skipped the Monitor and booted straight into Applesoft (or ran a
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #disk if present).
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #A couple of details worth knowing for your emulator:
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #1. Ctrl-B is just a Monitor command. The Monitor scans the input line for control commands;
241dff1f [kimi-k3] #`B` with the high bit set (`0x82`) triggers the "enter BASIC" routine. So your keyboard path
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #through `C000`/`C010` needs to deliver it like any other keypress — which, from your
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #`pushKey`/`keyBuffer` code, it looks like you're set up to do.
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #2. The reset vector flow. On reset, the old Monitor does its init, rings the bell, and
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #waits at `*`. If your `machine.ts` is reaching that point from the `apple2` ROM with just the
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #opcodes you've implemented so far, your 6502 coverage is already in decent shape — the Monitor
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #exercises a fair amount of the instruction set.
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #3. Handy test milestone: once Ctrl-B gets you the `>` prompt, the canonical smoke test is:
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #   >PRINT 3/2
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #   1
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #   If it prints `1` (integer truncation) rather than `1.5`, you're in genuine Integer BASIC, not
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #accidentally running Applesoft.
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #So: correct behaviour, keep it. If you later want the "plug in and it's already in BASIC"
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #experience, that would be historically wrong for this ROM — the II Plus / Autostart behaviour
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #belongs to `apple2plus` only.
241dff20 [kimi-k3] #
