241e2313 [kimi-k3] #One more: since you confirmed the Ctrl-B boot behaviour, a small "Getting In" note at the top would
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #be genuinely useful for anyone else (or future-you) reading this doc:
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #Boot to the Monitor (* prompt), then Ctrl-B, Enter to reach Integer BASIC (> prompt).
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #machine.ts
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #• The graphics path is stubbed but not wired up.* `displayAppleGraphics` and
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #`initAppleBlocks` are new, and the structure is right — a precomputed 256-entry lookup indexed by
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #byte, one `▀`-style entry per nybble pair. But:
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #1. `initAppleBlocks()` is never called, and the loop body assigns an empty `block` string
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #(and a `""` that TS will flag as missing a semicolon-tolerated expression — `const block=""` with
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #no semicolon then `appleBlock[i]=block` works, but every entry will be blank).
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #2. `displayAppleGraphics` is never called either — `tick()` still only calls `displayApple`
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #in the vblank path.
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #3. No state tracking for the soft switches. Reads of `C050`/`C051`/`C052`/`C056` currently
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #`return 0` without recording anything. You need flags like `gfxTextMode`, `gfxMixed`, `gfxLores`,
241e2313 [kimi-k3] #set in both `readAddress` and `writeAddress` (this is the part from last time that still
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #isn't fixed — writes to `C0xx` still halt the machine, and Integer BASIC's `GR` does `STA
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #C050`). Then `tick()` picks `displayApple` vs `displayAppleGraphics` based on those flags.
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #Suggested shape for the block table, once you wire it up:
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #▀ with fg = top-nybble colour, bg = bottom-nybble colour
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #ANSI 256-colour or truecolour escapes let you map the 16 Apple II colours onto
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #foreground/background pairs. Since there are only 256 byte values, precomputing full escape strings
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #at init (as you're doing) is exactly right for per-frame rendering speed.
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #• Small correctness notes on the lores layout* in `displayAppleGraphics`:
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #- The row-to-address math (`0x400 + 40(y>>3) + 128(y&7)`) is the text-screen mapping, which
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #is correct — lores shares the same screen holes. Good.
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #- Each of the 24 rows renders as two block-rows, so the graphics display will be 48 "lines" worth
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #of content — your terminal will need 48 rows for full-screen mode, or you render 20 rows of
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #graphics + 4 rows of text in mixed mode. The mixed-mode split (graphics rows 0–39 = text rows 0–19)
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #is where the real Apple II GR behaviour lives.
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #• The `interrupt()` IRQ vector* (from last review) is unchanged — still uses `$FFFC` for
241e2314 [kimi-k3] #the non-NMI case. Dormant for now, but worth a `// TODO` since Reset and IRQ sharing a vector will
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #bite when you enable vblank IRQs.
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #• `gameRom` is still `apple2plus` — so the machine you're booting is the
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #Autostart/Applesoft ROM, which contradicts the Ctrl-B-to-Integer-BASIC workflow you just verified.
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #If that verification was done on the plus ROM, what you actually saw was Applesoft's `]` prompt via
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #`Ctrl-B` from... hmm, actually the Autostart ROM doesn't have the `` Monitor workflow at all in
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #the same way. If you genuinely got `*` → Ctrl-B → `>` with Integer BASIC semantics (`PRINT 3/2` =
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #1), you must have been running `apple2`. Worth a quick check of which ROM produced the behaviour
241e2315 [kimi-k3] #you described, since the file says plus.
