241ec851 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.386 427.5KB 25.50s
241ec851 [kimi-k3] #`\x1b[0m` should reset foreground and background — it's SGR reset, which clears all
241ec851 [kimi-k3] #attributes including colours. So if your `cursorRestore` (`\x1b[0m`) isn't restoring colours, a few
241ec851 [kimi-k3] #possibilities:
241ec851 [kimi-k3] #
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #1. It's not being emitted where you think. In the new unified `displayApple`, I notice the
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #`console.log(cursorRestore)` at the end didn't survive the refactor — the old
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #`displayAppleGraphics` had it, but the merged function doesn't. If nothing after the graphics block
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #emits a reset, every cell's bg colour bleeds into the next line's default spaces, the scroll
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #region, etc. Check whether that's the actual cause before suspecting the escape code.
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #2. If you want targeted resets rather than full SGR reset:
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #   - `\x1b[39m` — default foreground
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #   - `\x1b[49m` — default background
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #   - `\x1b[39;49m` — both
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #   Useful when you want to keep other attributes (bold, etc.) but drop colours.
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #3. Terminal quirks. Some terminals (tmux with certain `TERM` entries, older Windows
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #consoles) have patchy `0m` handling. But macOS Terminal/iTerm2 handle it fine, so unlikely to be
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #your issue.
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #Also worth a habit: emit the reset per line at the end of each `line.join("")`, not just
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #once per frame. Otherwise any trailing background colour paints the rest of the row to the right
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #edge, and autowrap/newline inherits it. Per-frame reset alone leaves 24 rows' worth of right-edge
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #smear. So:
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #console.log(line.join("") + cursorRestore);
241ec852 [kimi-k3] #
241ec853 [kimi-k3] #
241ec853 [kimi-k3] #…or define a `sgrReset` constant if you want to keep `cursorRestore` for shutdown use only.
241ec853 [kimi-k3] #
