241ed2be [kimi-k3] #Nice — the soft switches now drive `displayMode` as a two-bit field, and `cursorRestore` sits
241ed2be [kimi-k3] #between the graphics and text sections. The bit-flag approach (`C050` sets bit 1, `C052/53`
241ed2be [kimi-k3] #sets/clears bit 0) is a neat compression of the state. A few things to check:
241ed2be [kimi-k3] #
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #• The enum now has a dummy entry. `DisplayMode{TEXT,TEXTTEXT,LORES,MIXED}` — because
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #`TEXT` is 0 and you need `MIXED` to be 3 (bits 0+1), the values are 0,1,2,3 where `TEXTTEXT`=1
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #occupies the "mixed bit set, graphics bit clear" combination. That's the undefined* hardware
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #state ($C053 mixed touched while in text mode), so a placeholder is honest — though worth a
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #comment, since `TEXTTEXT` reads like a typo to anyone else. Alternatively rename it something like
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #`UNUSED1`.
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #• Check the splitY table matches the enum values:*
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #const splitY=[0,0,20,24][mode];
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #- 0 TEXT → 0 graphics rows. Correct.
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #- 1 TEXTTEXT → 0. Undefined state treated as text. Fine.
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #- 2 LORES → 20. Wrong way round! LORES should be 24 graphics rows (full screen), MIXED
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #should be 20.
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #- 3 MIXED → 24. Should be 20.
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #So the table should be `[0,0,24,20]`. As written, `GR` (which produces mode 3) renders full-screen
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #graphics with no text window, and full-graphics mode 2 renders mixed. Easy transposition to make.
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #• Writes to C0xx still halt. Third review noting this — the `writeAddress` guard is
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #untouched. Integer BASIC's `GR` does `STA C050`, and on real hardware writes to soft
241ed2bf [kimi-k3] #switches work identically to reads. Right now the mode bits only change if the ROM reads* those
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #addresses. Some ROM routines do use `BIT` (read) to set switches, which is why your read path may
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #appear to work, but `STA`-based code will stop the machine. The switch cases in `readAddress` need
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #to be callable from `writeAddress` too — extract a `softSwitch(address)` method used by both paths.
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #• The cursorRestore placement* is now between the two sections — good, it prevents graphics
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #colours bleeding into the text window. The earlier per-line suggestion still applies for the
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #right-edge smear, but if the display looks clean in practice, don't chase it.
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #• Minor:* `displayMode:number` on the class could be typed `DisplayMode` now that the enum
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #exists. And the big comment block documenting the $C05x mapping above `readAddress` is useful —
241ed2c0 [kimi-k3] #keep it, though it slightly duplicates the inline `// gfx:` comments.
