241edb08 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.472 522.1KB 35.15s
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #You're not missing anything — the flow is exactly:
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #GR
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #COLOR=1
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #PLOT 0,0
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #`COLOR=` is an assignment statement, not a function call — you write `COLOR=1` (or
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #`COLOR=15` etc.), with values 0–15 selecting from the lores palette. It sets the colour that
241edb08 [kimi-k3] #subsequent `PLOT`, `HLIN`, and `VLIN` use.
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #A couple of notes for your testing:
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #- After `GR` alone, the drawing colour is whatever was last set — on a fresh boot, 0 (black). So
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #`PLOT` right after `GR` draws black-on-black: it works, you just can't see it. If your
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #emulator shows nothing after `GR` + `PLOT 0,0`, the first suspect is "you plotted black", the
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #second is the mode bits.
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #- `COLOR` persists across `PLOT`s, so `COLOR=1: PLOT 0,0: PLOT 1,1` plots two magenta blocks.
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #- A good smoke-test line that exercises GR, COLOR, and HLIN in one go, typed at the `>` prompt:
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #GR: COLOR=1: HLIN 0,39 AT 0: COLOR=12: HLIN 0,39 AT 1
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #That should give you a magenta bar across the top and a green one just under it, with the text
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #window still alive at the bottom (mode 3, once the splitY table is fixed).
241edb09 [kimi-k3] #
