241eeb9d [simon@midnightblue.local] how does hires work?
241eede4 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.484 527.7KB 36.03s
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #Hires is a different beast entirely — different memory, different encoding, different everything.
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #Here's the shape of it, and importantly, how much of it applies to your Integer BASIC goals:
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #• Memory layout. Hires page 1 lives at `2000`–`3FFF` (8 KB), page 2 at `4000`–`5FFF`. It's
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #a bit-mapped display: 280 × 192 pixels*, one bit per pixel, seven pixels per byte. The
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #eighth bit (bit 7) doesn't draw — it shifts the colour phase of that byte's seven pixels (the
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #infamous "colour set" bit).
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #• Addressing is weirder than text.* The rows interleave in groups of 8 with a 1 KB stride
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #pattern. Row `y`'s address is:
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #base + (y & 7) * 1024 + ((y >> 3) & 7) * 128 + (y >> 6) * 40 + (x >> 3...
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #— honestly, nobody computes it by hand; everyone uses the ROM routine or a lookup table. The
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #formula: `addr = 2000 + (y%8)400 + ((y/8)%8)80 + (y/64)*28 + x/7`. Worth verifying against
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #the Red Book when you implement it; it's the classic source of off-by-one-row bugs.
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #• Colour is a side effect, not data.* This is the part everyone trips on. The Apple II's
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #hires colours emerge from NTSC artifacting: pixels in even vs odd columns produce different
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #colours, and bit 7 flips which set:
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #
241eede5 [kimi-k3] #- bit 7 clear: even columns ≈ purple, odd columns ≈ green
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #- bit 7 set: even columns ≈ blue, odd columns ≈ orange
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #- adjacent 11 pixel pairs: white; adjacent 00: black
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #So six colours, but only via a 2-pixel effective resolution for colour, and with fringing
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #artifacts. Your `▀`-block terminal trick doesn't map onto this at all — you'd be rendering at the
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #pixel level (280×192), e.g. with two vertical pixels per `▀` cell at 140×96 half-block cells, or
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #doubling horizontally.
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #• The switches.* `C057` (hires on), `C056` (lores on), plus the same `C050/51`
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #graphics/text and `C052/53` full/mixed, and `$C054/55` page. Mixed hires keeps the 4-line text
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #window over the bottom 32 pixel rows.
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #• Now, the good news: Integer BASIC has no hires commands.* `HGR`, `HPLOT`, `HCOLOR` are
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #Applesoft additions. On Integer BASIC, hires was reached only via `POKE`ing the soft switches and
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #the bitmap directly (or `CALL`ing ROM routines). So for Brick Out and the language on your markdown
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #list, hires is out of scope. The soft-switch handling you're already building (`$C057` case) can
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #just set a flag for now, and hires rendering can be a separate milestone when you care about the
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #Applesoft/hires-demo catalogue.
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #If you do implement it later, the honest recommendation: render it as monochrome first (280×192,
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #white-on-black), get the addressing right, and add artifact colour afterwards — many Apple II
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #programs were written for and look correct on the monochrome interpretation.
241eede6 [kimi-k3] #
