241f00ac [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.483 530.8KB 34.00s
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #You're right on both counts, and that quad-character string is already sitting in your file as
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #`gridQuads` — so the idea fits your codebase nicely. Two vertical pixels per cell is what `▀` gives
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #you; going to quads (`▘▝▖▗` etc., 2×2 pixels per cell) gets you 140 × 96 cells, which is
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #exactly half-resolution hires and maps 2×2 pixel blocks perfectly onto one glyph. That's the
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #natural terminal encoding for hires.
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #And you're correct about monochrome being misleading. Even the period "green screen" monitors
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #weren't showing raw bits for colour content — the NTSC artifact colours became distinct
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #luminance/dot patterns (purple vs green have different densities and phases of on-pixels), so
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #software that relied on colour was still legible, just texture-y. A flat white-on-black
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #monochrome render would actually be less faithful than the patterned reality — dithered
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #regions would flicker between solid white and half-density depending on phase.
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #Practical options when you get there, roughly in effort order:
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #1. 6-colour simulation at 140×96 quads — decode each pair of adjacent pixels + column
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #parity + bit-7 phase into the 6 hires colours, treat each 2×2 block's colours as the cell's pixels.
241f00ac [kimi-k3] #Colours within a quad cell can differ per sub-pixel, but the quad glyph is monochrome, so you'd
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #need fg/bg per cell — meaning quantising 4 sub-pixels to 2 colours. Lossy but readable.
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #2. 140×96 with `▀` and true fg/bg — 1×2 sub-pixels per cell, each cell shows exactly 2
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #horizontally-adjacent pixels with two colours. This is the clean mapping: each `▀` = one
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #even/odd pixel pair, which is precisely the unit of hires colour. No quantisation loss at all. 140
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #columns is wide for a terminal but workable.
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #3. Monochrome with phase-aware dithering — render on/off pixels faithfully per the colour
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #artifact patterns. Most faithful to a period green-screen, but as you say, "useless" for actually
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #seeing which colour was intended.
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #Option 2 is the sweet spot when the time comes: it's the same `▀` machinery you've already built
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #for lores, just driven from `$2000` with a different address decoder and a 6-entry palette indexed
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #by (parity, phase, pixel pair).
241f00ad [kimi-k3] #
241f0e34 [simon@midnightblue.local] in deno with console in raw mode can i still support system clipboard
241f0ff9 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.484 533.2KB 27.83s
241f0ff9 [kimi-k3] #Short answer: reading yes, writing no — with caveats on the reading.
241f0ff9 [kimi-k3] #
241f0ff9 [kimi-k3] #Deno has `navigator.clipboard`, gated behind the `--allow-clipboard-read` /
241f0ff9 [kimi-k3] #`--allow-clipboard-write` permission flags (or `--allow-clipboard` for both). Raw stdin mode
241f0ff9 [kimi-k3] #doesn't interfere with the API itself — it's a Deno runtime feature, independent of TTY state.
241f0ff9 [kimi-k3] #
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #The catch is in how the two halves behave:
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #• `navigator.clipboard.readText()` — works, but it's how you poll the clipboard.*
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #On macOS/Linux this reads the system clipboard content as a string. So if your goal is "user copies
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #something in another app, then pastes into my Apple II" (e.g. typing a BASIC listing in via
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #clipboard rather than keystrokes), you can read it on demand — say, bound to a hotkey in your
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #`onInput` handler — and then feed it through `pushKey` character by character. That path is fully
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #workable today and is actually a really nice fit for your project: pasting period magazine listings
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #straight into Integer BASIC.
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #• `navigator.clipboard.writeText()` — also works*, and raw mode doesn't block it either. So
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #"copy the Apple II screen contents to the system clipboard" (e.g. `LIST` output capture) is
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #feasible too.
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #• What raw mode does take away: the terminal's own selection/clipboard
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #integration is unaffected (that's the terminal emulator's feature, not yours — selecting text in
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #iTerm2 with the mouse still works since your app never sees it). What you can't* rely on is
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #Ctrl+C/Ctrl+V reaching you as "copy/paste" — in raw mode those arrive as bytes (`0x03`, `0x16`),
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #and it's up to you to interpret them. So the wiring is: catch your chosen hotkey byte in `onInput`,
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #call the clipboard API, route the result into the emulated keyboard.
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #One gotcha: the Apple II keyboard expects uppercase with the high bit set in your `pushKey` model —
241f0ffa [kimi-k3] #a pasted lowercase listing will need uppercasing (and Integer BASIC only accepts uppercase keywords
241f0ffb [kimi-k3] #anyway, so that's historically appropriate).
241f0ffb [kimi-k3] #
241f357c [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.563 625.9KB 38.44s
241f357c [kimi-k3] #The clipboard work has begun — `pasteMachine` exists and there's a placeholder `"PASTEKEYHERE"`
241f357c [kimi-k3] #case in the F-key handler. A few problems to flag before it bites:
241f357c [kimi-k3] #
241f357c [kimi-k3] #• 1. `pasteMachine` iterates the string wrong.*
241f357c [kimi-k3] #
241f357c [kimi-k3] #for (const keyCode of text) {
241f357c [kimi-k3] #	game.pushKey(keyCode);
241f357c [kimi-k3] #}
241f357c [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #`for...of` over a string yields characters (strings), not numbers, but `pushKey` expects a
241f357d [kimi-k3] #`number`. TypeScript should complain (`string` not assignable to `number`) — unless it compiled
241f357d [kimi-k3] #because `keyCode` is untyped somewhere. Either way you want:
241f357d [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #for (const ch of text) {
241f357d [kimi-k3] #	game.pushKey(ch.toUpperCase().charCodeAt(0));
241f357d [kimi-k3] #}
241f357d [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #The `toUpperCase()` matters because Integer BASIC rejects lowercase input, as discussed. Also
241f357d [kimi-k3] #consider translating `\n` → `\r` (13): the Apple II expects carriage return, and clipboard text has
241f357d [kimi-k3] #Unix newlines.
241f357d [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #• 2. `navigator.clipboard.readText()` is async.* It returns a `Promise<string>`, and you're
241f357d [kimi-k3] #passing the promise straight into `pasteMachine`. The handler needs to be async:
241f357d [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #const paste = await navigator.clipboard.readText();
241f357d [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #which means the enclosing arrow/switch context needs `await` support — `onInput` would become
241f357d [kimi-k3] #`async`, and the loop calling it should `await onInput(bytes)`. Alternatively fire-and-forget with
241f357d [kimi-k3] #`.then()`. Without awaiting, you'll push the string `"[object Promise]"` character by character —
241f357d [kimi-k3] #which will at least be entertaining the first time.
241f357d [kimi-k3] #
241f357d [kimi-k3] #• 3. `case "PASTEKEYHERE":` is unreachable and a type error.* The surrounding `switch`
241f357e [kimi-k3] #matches on `bytes.subarray(...).join(",")` — a string of comma-separated numbers like
241f357e [kimi-k3] #`"27,91,50,52,126"`. A case label of `"PASTEKEYHERE"` never matches. You need to pick an actual
241f357e [kimi-k3] #F-key sequence and put its numeric form there. F11 in many terminals sends `ESC [ 2 3 ~` →
241f357e [kimi-k3] #`"27,91,50,51,126"`. So:
241f357e [kimi-k3] #
241f357e [kimi-k3] #
241f357e [kimi-k3] #case "27,91,50,51,126": { ... paste logic ... }
241f357e [kimi-k3] #
241f357e [kimi-k3] #
241f357e [kimi-k3] #• 4. Deno permissions.* You'll need `--allow-clipboard-read` on the command line (and
241f357e [kimi-k3] #`--allow-clipboard-write` later if you add screen-copy). Without it the promise rejects — so wrap
241f357e [kimi-k3] #in try/catch and log, or the unhandled rejection takes the machine down mid-paste.
241f357e [kimi-k3] #
241f357e [kimi-k3] #• 5. Pace the paste.* Your `keyBuffer` mechanism drains as the ROM polls `$C000`, which
241f357e [kimi-k3] #should naturally throttle — good design. But a 500-line paste will take a while at keyboard polling
241f357e [kimi-k3] #speed; that's authentic and fine, just expect it.
