241f42c2 [simon@midnightblue.local] do deno does not support clipboard?
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.567 628.7KB 26.50s
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #Deno does support clipboard — `navigator.clipboard.readText()` and `writeText()` are real,
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #available by default in recent Deno versions, gated behind `--allow-clipboard-read` /
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #`--allow-clipboard-write` permission flags.
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #My point 4 wasn't "no support", it was: if you run without the flag, the promise rejects
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #with a permission error at call time. Deno may also interactively prompt for permission if stdin is
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #a TTY — but your stdin is in raw mode being pumped by `pollInput`, so the prompt could behave
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #oddly. Pass the flag explicitly and it's a non-issue:
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #deno run --allow-clipboard-read machine.ts
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #(plus whatever flags you already use — `--allow-read`/`--allow-write` for the WAV files, and
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #`--unstable-*` flags if any of your `system.ts` APIs need them.)
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #So the API is fine; the actual fixes needed in your code are points 1–3 from before: iterate
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #characters with `charCodeAt`, `await` the promise, and replace `"PASTEKEYHERE"` with a real
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #escape-sequence match.
241f4473 [kimi-k3] #
2420726f [simon@midnightblue.local] when not in raw mode i need to wait for return key for all keyboard input?
24207480 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.520 630.0KB 32.56s
24207480 [kimi-k3] #Yes — in cooked (non-raw) mode, the terminal line-discipline buffers input and only
24207480 [kimi-k3] #delivers it to your program when Enter is pressed. That's the OS/TTY layer, not Deno: `Deno.stdin`
24207480 [kimi-k3] #reads in cooked mode get whole lines at a time, with line editing (backspace etc.) handled by the
24207480 [kimi-k3] #terminal itself.
24207480 [kimi-k3] #
24207480 [kimi-k3] #That's why your `runInput(false)` presumably puts the TTY into raw mode — without it:
24207480 [kimi-k3] #
24207480 [kimi-k3] #- single keys like your `D`/`R`/`S`/`T` debug commands wouldn't fire until you typed the key
24207480 [kimi-k3] #plus Enter;
24207480 [kimi-k3] #- arrow/F-key escape sequences would arrive all at once after Enter, batched into one read;
24207480 [kimi-k3] #- and Ctrl+C would kill the process (SIGINT) instead of arriving as byte `0x03`.
24207480 [kimi-k3] #
24207480 [kimi-k3] #So raw mode is the right choice for a machine emulator where keystrokes are events. The trade-offs
24207481 [kimi-k3] #you already have in hand:
24207481 [kimi-k3] #
24207481 [kimi-k3] #- You're responsible for echo (you don't echo — the emulated Apple II "echoes" via its screen
24207481 [kimi-k3] #render, which is the correct behaviour anyway);
24207481 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+C no longer kills the app — you handle quit yourself (plain ESC → `running=false`);
24207481 [kimi-k3] #- Pasting into the terminal delivers the paste as a byte stream you feed to `pushKey` — same as
24207481 [kimi-k3] #your planned clipboard path.
24207481 [kimi-k3] #
24207481 [kimi-k3] #One refinement worth knowing about: bracketed paste mode. If you enable it (`\x1b[?2004h`
24207481 [kimi-k3] #on startup, `\x1b[?2004l` on shutdown), terminals wrap genuine user pastes in markers (`\x1b[200~`
24207481 [kimi-k3] #… `\x1b[201~`). That lets you distinguish "user pasted 200 bytes" from "user typed fast", so you
24207481 [kimi-k3] #can route terminal pastes through `pasteMachine` automatically without a hotkey, and without the OS
24207481 [kimi-k3] #clipboard API at all. That's often the nicer solution for terminal apps — the user's normal Cmd+V
24207481 [kimi-k3] #just works. The Deno permission flag and the `readText()` path remain useful as a fallback for
24207481 [kimi-k3] #terminals that don't support bracketed paste.
24207481 [kimi-k3] #
24208916 [simon@midnightblue.local] ctrl c causes sigint in cook mode, any other keys that cause instant behavior ?
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.574 632.0KB 22.58s
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #Yes — in cooked mode the TTY line discipline intercepts several control characters before your
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #program ever sees them. The main set on POSIX systems (macOS included):
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #• Signal-generating keys* (process-level, instant):
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+C → SIGINT (interrupt)
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+\\ → SIGQUIT (quit + core dump)
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+Z → SIGTSTP (suspend to background — your app freezes, shell shows `^Z`, `fg`
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #resumes)
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+Y → SIGTSTP, but deferred until the process tries to read it (rarely
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #enabled)
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #• Line-editing keys* (consumed by the terminal, never reach you):
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Backspace/Delete (VERASE) — erases a character from the line buffer
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+W (VWERASE) — erase previous word
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+U (VKILL) — erase the whole line
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+R (VREPRINT) — reprint the line
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+V (VLNEXT) — literal-next: makes the following control char literal
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+O (VDISCARD) — discard pending output
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #
24208aa5 [kimi-k3] #• Flow control* (affects the pipe itself):
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+S (VSTOP) — pauses output. This is the nasty one: your emulator's
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #`console.log` calls block, the machine appears frozen, and there's no on-screen hint why.
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+Q (VSTART) — resumes output.
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #Ctrl+S/Q (XON/XOFF) deserve emphasis because they surprise people: an emulator that "randomly
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #freezes" when someone hits Ctrl+S, and "unfreezes" mysteriously with Ctrl+Q.
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #Two more worth knowing:
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+D (VEOF) — on an empty line, makes `read()` return 0 bytes (EOF), which your
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #`pollInput` would likely surface as `null` → your code sets `running=false`. So Ctrl+D is a
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #cooked-mode "quit" for you.
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #- Ctrl+M / Enter — delivers the line (that's the whole buffering mechanism).
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #All of these are configurable via `stty` (e.g. `stty intr ^T` moves SIGINT off Ctrl+C, `stty -ixon`
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #disables Ctrl+S/Q flow control), but raw mode sidesteps the entire table at once — which is the
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #standard practice for emulators, editors, and anything else wanting keystroke-level control.
24208aa6 [kimi-k3] #
