242263e7 [simon@midnightblue.local] with appleii hires mode, how is memory used
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.051 513B 94.67s
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #Where it lives
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #Hi-res mode uses 8 KB per page, with two pages available:
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #- Page 1: `2000`–`3FFF`
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #- Page 2: `4000`–`5FFF`
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #You select the page and mode with soft switches in the `$C05x` range (page 1/2, graphics/text,
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #lo-res/hi-res, full/mixed screen). Two pages allow page-flipping for flicker-free animation.
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #Byte-to-pixel mapping
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #- Resolution is 280 × 192, but each byte holds only 7 pixels (bits 0–6), with 40 bytes per
242269f6 [kimi-k3] #scanline.
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #- Bit 7 doesn't display a pixel — it shifts the color phase. With the high bit clear you get the
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #violet/green pair; with it set, blue/orange. Whether a set bit shows as the first or second color
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #of the pair depends on whether it falls in an even or odd column. Two adjacent set bits blend into
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #white; clear bits are black. All of this is NTSC artifact color — on a monochrome monitor you just
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #get clean dots.
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #- 40 bytes × 192 rows = 7680 bytes displayed, leaving 512 bytes of "screen holes" (64 gaps
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #of 8 bytes each) that are invisible and often used as scratch space by programs and peripheral
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #cards.
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #The interleaved layout
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #Rows are not stored consecutively. Wozniak arranged addressing so the video circuitry's counters
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #could double as the DRAM refresh counters. The address of pixel row Y is:
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #`page_base + (Y mod 8)×400 + ((Y div 8) mod 8)×80 + (Y div 64)×$28 + X`
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #Practical consequences:
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #- The screen is split into three vertical bands of 64 rows each, and within a band the rows jump
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #around in `$400` steps.
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #- Plotting a single pixel requires a read-modify-write of the byte containing it (or XOR for fast
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #drawing).
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #- Most graphics routines use a precomputed table of 192 row base addresses rather than calculating
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #the formula each time.
242269f7 [kimi-k3] #
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #Mixed mode
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #With the mixed-screen switch set, the bottom 4 lines show text pulled from text page 1
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #(`400`–`7FF`), while the top 160 lines show hi-res graphics — that's what gives you the familiar
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #command line under a drawing.
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #Interaction with BASIC
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #Applesoft programs load at `$801` and grow upward, so the pages eat into program space:
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #- `HGR` (page 1): your program and variables must fit below `$2000` (~6 KB usable)
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #- `HGR2` (page 2): everything must fit below `$4000`
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #This is why larger programs often need `HIMEM:` adjustments or poke the screen directly instead of
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #using Applesoft's shape tables.
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #Worth knowing
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #- On the IIe and later, double hi-res adds the auxiliary memory bank: another 8 KB shadow
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #page, giving 560 × 192 effective resolution and 16 colors, with bytes interleaved between main and
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #aux memory.
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #- The lo-res/text pages (`400`–`BFF`) use exactly the same interleaved addressing scheme in
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #miniature, which is why the two modes share so much circuitry.
242269f8 [kimi-k3] #
242522f1 [simon@midnightblue.local] are the bits msb or lsb first?
24252542 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.016 3.471KB 34.97s
24252542 [kimi-k3] #LSB first — bit 0 is the leftmost pixel of the byte's 7-pixel group, bit 6 the rightmost:
24252542 [kimi-k3] #
24252542 [kimi-k3] #
24252542 [kimi-k3] #bit:      7   6   5   4   3   2   1   0
24252542 [kimi-k3] #pixel:  (hue) 7th 6th 5th 4th 3rd 2nd 1st   ← left to right
24252542 [kimi-k3] #
24252542 [kimi-k3] #
24252542 [kimi-k3] #So a byte value of `01` lights the leftmost dot, `40` the rightmost, and `$80` alone shows nothing
24252542 [kimi-k3] #but shifts the palette for any set bits in that byte.
24252542 [kimi-k3] #
24252542 [kimi-k3] #This feels backwards if you're used to MSB-first formats (like most bitmap formats), but it matches
24252543 [kimi-k3] #the hardware: the video generator is a shift register clocking bits out in that order, which was
24252543 [kimi-k3] #the simplest design for Woz to implement.
24252543 [kimi-k3] #
24252543 [kimi-k3] #Practical consequence for plotting: for pixel X on a row, the byte is at column `X div 7` and you
24252543 [kimi-k3] #set bit `X mod 7` — then optionally set bit 7 if you need the shifted color pair. This is why
24252543 [kimi-k3] #Applesoft's `HPLOT` and most assembly plotting routines use a lookup table of bit masks (`01, 02,
24252543 [kimi-k3] #04 ... 40`) indexed by `X mod 7`.
24252543 [kimi-k3] #
