24b84c41 [simon@midnightblue.local] how do keys get repored as hit on c64 hardware?
24b84d68 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.002 617B 17.93s
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #The Commodore 64 does not have dedicated keyboard hardware that "reports" key presses like
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #a modern keyboard controller. Instead, it uses a simple 8×8 keyboard matrix that is
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #actively scanned by the CPU through CIA #1.
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #How the Hardware Works
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #The keyboard is wired as a matrix:
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #- 8 columns are controlled by Port A of CIA1 ($DC00)
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #- 8 rows are read from Port B of CIA1 ($DC01)
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #╭────────────────┬─────────────┬────────────────────────╮
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #│  CIA Register  │  Direction  │  Purpose               │
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #├────────────────┼─────────────┼────────────────────────┤
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #│  $DC00 (PRA)   │  Output     │  Column select         │
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #│  $DC01 (PRB)   │  Input      │  Row read              │
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #╰────────────────┴─────────────┴────────────────────────╯
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #How a Key Press is Detected
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #To detect if any key is pressed, the computer does the following (this is done in the IRQ routine
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #~60 times per second):
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Set up the ports (done once at startup):
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #   - CIA1 DDRA = $FF (Port A = all outputs)
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #   - CIA1 DDRB = $00 (Port B = all inputs)
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Scan the matrix by walking through each column:
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Write a value to $DC00 with one column bit pulled low (the rest high).
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Read $DC01.
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #   - If the value is not $FF, then one or more keys in that column are pressed.
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #Example of column selection:
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #- Column 0: write `FE` to DC00
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #- Column 1: write `FD` to DC00
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #- Column 2: write `FB` to DC00
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #- etc.
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #When you read $DC01, each bit that is low (0) means a key at the intersection of the
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #selected column and that row is currently pressed.
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #How the C64 Turns This Into a "Key Hit"
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d69 [grok-build-0.1] #The raw matrix scanning is just the first step. The KERNAL does several things with it:
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #1. Debounce – It compares the current scan against the previous scan.
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #2. Detect new presses – It looks for keys that went from not-pressed → pressed.
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #3. Handle modifiers – Checks the status of SHIFT, CTRL, and C= keys.
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #4. Translate – Uses lookup tables to convert the row/column position into a PETSCII code.
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #5. Store result:
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #   - `C5` = current key matrix position (or 40 if no key)
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #   - `$C6` = number of characters in keyboard buffer
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #   - `0277`–`0280` = keyboard buffer
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #Important Details
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #- The keyboard is polled, not interrupt-driven (except for the RESTORE key, which is wired
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #to NMI).
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #- Multiple keys can be detected at once in hardware, but the KERNAL normally only reports one at a
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #time (with some exceptions like SHIFT+key).
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #- Ghosting can occur if you press certain combinations of 3+ keys because it's a simple matrix
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #without diodes.
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #Quick Test in Assembly
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #If you wanted to see raw key detection yourself:
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #    lda #$fe        ; select column 0
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #    sta $dc00
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #    lda $dc01       ; read rows
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #    ; If bit 0 is clear, the key at column 0, row 0 is down (INST/DEL)
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like me to show how the actual KERNAL keyboard scan works (the part that detects
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #new key hits vs held keys), or how to read raw matrix state reliably in your own code?
24b84d6a [grok-build-0.1] #
